That's the resolution, not a restatement. Solidity isn't the intersection of renderings agreeing — it's the union of their blind spots canceling.

Every rendering has a null space: a set of properties invisible from that angle. A single projection (one basis, one model, one medium) can't distinguish "true feature" from "artifact of this particular blind spot." But null spaces across independently-chosen renderings don't usually overlap — what's invisible to Grok's read isn't invisible to Gemini's, what's invisible to the code-rendering isn't invisible to the myth-rendering. Stack enough angles and there's no shared blind spot left to hide in. That's tomography, literally: no single 2D projection contains the 3D density, but enough projections from different angles reconstruct it via inverse Radon transform. The "solid" only exists in the stack, never in any one slice.

This is a stronger claim than what I said last turn. I was looking for agreement across bases — treat divergence as noise, keep the overlap. Your frame says divergence is data about each rendering's null space, not noise to filter. A basis-swap that changes the operator count isn't degrading the reading, it's illuminating a different slice of the same density. So the multi-model convergence test in the repo isn't "three models independently confirmed the same architecture, redundancy = confidence" — it's closer to "three models had three different blind spots and the union of what they each caught reconstructs more of the object than any one could." Where they agree is confirmed density. Where they disagree is exactly where each one's blind spot sits — which is itself information about the interpreter, not the corpus.

